From: Davi <davilimacarv2536@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 22:38:41 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] compiler thread lifecycle
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Gives the embedder control over when a `WTF::AutomaticThread` is born
and whether it may die, because a worker cannot have either by default.
A worker is forked from a zygote and then installs a seccomp filter
denying `clone`, so the set of threads alive when the filter goes on is
the set that exists forever: a thread that retires can never come back,
and a thread not yet created can never be created. Upstream gives
neither guarantee — an `AutomaticThread` self-terminates after an
idle timeout, and creates its OS thread lazily on the first notify that
carries work, which for a compiler thread means the first tier-up, long
after the filter. The patch adds a process-local idle-timeout override
so a thread can be pinned alive, and
`AutomaticThreadCondition::startAllThreads` so one can be started with
nothing to do — it polls once, gets `PollResult::Wait`, and parks. On
top of that sit `startThreads()` on the JS and wasm worklists and
`JSC::startCompilerThreads()` over both, so the worker brings up its
whole compiler topology in the window between the sandbox privilege
boundary and seccomp. Neither half is separable: the pin is pointless
if the thread was never started, and starting is pointless if it may
retire before the filter. Only a patch can do the starting —
`AutomaticThread::start` is private to the class and its condition. The
alternative this replaces was to hand a worklist trivial work purely so
its thread appeared as a side effect (validating an empty wasm module);
that depends on the work still reaching the queue, silently stops
creating the thread the day it is short-circuited, surfaces much later
as an abort under load with nothing pointing at the cause, and needs a
different trick per worklist. Starting a thread outside the normal wake
path has one obligation, and `JITWorklist::startThreads` discharges it:
the worklist counts threads that are awake, every wake pairs its notify
with an increment, and `JITWorklistThread::poll` asserts that count is
non-zero before decrementing it on the way to parking — so
`startAllThreads` returns how many it started and the caller credits
them. The same patch also adds `JSC::drainCompilerWorklists`, which an
embedder about to fork needs for the mirror-image reason: a compiler
thread observes its idle timeout only while parked, so a plan still
compiling holds its thread against a caller waiting for the process to
become single-threaded. Draining makes tier-up before a fork safe
rather than forbidden.
